// Runtime/ProcessRuntime.maxon — the RUNNING image, asked about and acted on.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT IT SERVES — THREE `__Builtins` INTRINSICS ABOUT THE RUNNING PROCESS.**
// `stdlib/Process.maxon`'s `executablePath()` bottoms out in `__Builtins.executablePath()`;
// `Compiler/TreeLock.maxon`'s lock token bottoms out in `__Builtins.currentProcessId()`; and
// `Main.runSpecTest` calls `__Builtins.enterBackgroundPriority()` before it spawns a worker pool. Only the
// first has a stdlib wrapper: a declaration whose whole body is one intrinsic call is the thin wrapper the
// house rules forbid. Nothing else in the language reaches these entry points. The bootstrap declares all
// three (`2-Parser.cs:11265-11267`, `:12238-12243`, and the `enterBackgroundPriority` entry beside
// `cpuCount`) with the same arities and result types, so the SURFACE here is the reference's; only the
// implementation differs.
//
// ⚠ **THE THIRD ONE IS NOT A QUESTION, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE IN THIS FILE THAT IS NOT.** The first two
// report; `enterBackgroundPriority` CHANGES the process and then reports, which is why its answer is a
// read-back rather than an echo of what it wrote — see `StdOp.osEnterBackgroundPriority`.
//
// ⚠ **EACH IS INSTALLED ON ITS OWN `RuntimeUsage` BIT, NOT ON ONE SHARED `__proc_` BIT**, for the reason the
// console/process SPLIT itself exists (`Compiler.maxon`'s install block: *"each on its OWN bit, so a program
// that reads stdin carries no path walk"*). A pid is three machine instructions and touches no memory; the
// path walk is a doubling retry loop that drags in `usesHeap` AND `usesManagedMemory` — i.e. the WHOLE
// managed-memory runtime, installed as one closure. Keyed together, `__Builtins.currentProcessId()` alone
// would have pulled all of that into a program that allocates nothing — and the same now holds for
// `__proc_bg_priority`, which is likewise a handful of instructions that touch no memory.
//
// ⭐ **HOST-ONLY BY THE R4 RULING (2026-07-30), AND FOR A REASON OF ITS OWN ON TOP.** `x64-windows` is real;
// every other target refuses at the call site with the EXISTING `E3104 targetUnsupportedConstruct` via
// `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`, which matches on the `__proc_` PREFIX — so an entry point added
// later is gated by construction rather than by memory.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE EXTRA REASON MATTERS, because it is not "nobody wrote the lowering yet": THERE ARE THREE
// DIFFERENT OS APIS AND THEY DO NOT AGREE ON THEIR OWN SHAPE.** `stdlib/Process.maxon:26-27` names all
// three — `GetModuleFileNameA` on Windows, `_NSGetExecutablePath` on macOS, `/proc/self/exe` on Linux — and
// they differ in more than spelling: the first FILLS a caller's buffer and answers a count, the second takes
// an IN-OUT size and signals "too small" through a `-1` return plus a rewritten size, the third is a
// SYMLINK that must be `readlink`'d and is not NUL-terminated. Those are three retry protocols behind one
// surface, and shv2's Std tier is TARGET-NEUTRAL: there is no place in it for a body that is a
// doubling loop on one target and a readlink on another. Serving them honestly means a per-target primitive
// plus a target-neutral surface over it — a rung, not a lowering.
// ⚠ wasm is refused for a STRONGER reason than any of those: WASI has no executable-path primitive AT ALL.
// A component has no image path to report, and `args_get[0]` is whatever the host chose to pass rather than
// a resolved path — so a lowering there could only be a plausible WRONG ANSWER, which is worse than a
// refusal because `File.exists` on it would simply say false and the program would blame itself.

// The `__proc_` prefix that identifies EVERY process-introspection runtime entry point — the one test
// `scanRuntimeUsage` reads to set `usesProcessIntrospection`, and the one `SemanticCheck` reads for E3104.
let ProcessRuntimePrefix = b"__proc_"

// ⭐ **THE THREE ENTRY POINTS.**
export let ProcessExecutablePathName = b"__proc_exe_path"
export let ProcessCurrentPidName = b"__proc_pid"
// ⭐ **THE THIRD, AND THE PREFIX PROMISE COLLECTED A SECOND TIME.** It joins the `__proc_` band, so
// `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses it on every non-x64-windows target with no edit there —
// exactly as `__proc_pid` did when it arrived. See `isProcessRuntimeCallee`.
export let ProcessBackgroundPriorityName = b"__proc_bg_priority"

// The first buffer the walk asks for is `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.MaxPathBytes` — the same `MAX_PATH` the
// `GetCurrentDirectoryA` walk starts at, READ from there rather than re-spelled here (W5 review), because
// what 260 means is a Win32 fact and not this loop's policy. `MAX_PATH` fits every path a program is
// normally installed at, so the overwhelmingly common case is one call and one allocation — and it is not a
// LIMIT here the way it is in an API that refuses past it, because the loop below grows past it. The
// bootstrap starts at 512 (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:1189`); the smaller start costs at most one extra
// doubling on a long path and allocates half as much on every ordinary one.

// The growth factor, as the shift that applies it. Named because a bare `1` at a `shl` says nothing about
// which of the two obvious policies (double, or ask the OS for the size) this loop uses — and for
// `GetModuleFileNameA` the second is NOT available, which is the whole reason the loop exists.
let PathGrowthShift = 1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **`__proc_exe_path() -> arrRec`** — a FRESH owned `__ManagedMemory` holding the running image's
// absolute path, and the caller frees it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A REAL LOOP, WHERE `__md_current_path`'s RETRY IS STRAIGHT-LINE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE
// API's NOT THE AUTHOR's.** `GetCurrentDirectoryA` answers the size it NEEDS when the buffer is too small,
// so one retry at exactly that size is provably enough and a second failure would be the OS contradicting
// itself. `GetModuleFileNameA` does not: on truncation it answers `nSize` — the size it was GIVEN — so
// there is no size to retry at, only a bigger guess. Doubling is therefore the only available policy and
// the number of doublings is not bounded by anything this code knows, which makes a loop the honest
// structure rather than a stylistic choice. (The bootstrap loops too, for the same reason.)
//
// ⭐ **THE THREE ANSWERS ARE CLASSIFIED BY THE SHARED VERDICT, NOT BY A SECOND COPY OF IT** (W5 review).
// `0` is a failure; `< capacity` means the path is in the buffer, NUL-terminated; anything else means
// truncation — and `written == 0` must be asked FIRST, because a 0 also satisfies `< capacity`. That is one
// fact about a Win32 buffer fill and it now lives in one place,
// `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.emitFilledBufferVerdict`; only where each of the three answers GOES is this
// loop's own. Here the third grows and goes round again, where `__md_current_path`'s retries once at the
// size the OS named.
//
// ⚠ **A FAILURE ANSWERS THE EMPTY BUFFER RATHER THAN THROWING**, which is the intrinsic's documented
// contract (`/specs/builtins-type.md`: *"empty buffer when unavailable"*) and matches `__cl_arg`, whose
// out-of-range index is answered rather than refused. It is spelled by NUL-terminating the buffer at
// offset 0 and then joining the ORDINARY copy-out — one producer of the answer, whatever the reason for it,
// which is exactly how `__cl_arg` spells its own out-of-range case.
// ⚠ It is also unreachable in practice on this target: `GetModuleFileNameA(NULL, …)` asks about the module
// that is currently executing, and the only documented failure is a buffer too small — which the loop
// answers rather than surfaces. The arm is here because "the OS cannot fail" is not something a runtime
// gets to assume, not because a test drives it.
//
// ⚠ The raw buffer is a scratch allocation this function owns end to end: it is freed on the one exit, and
// what the caller receives is the independent `__ManagedMemory` copy.
function buildProcessExecutablePath(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ProcessExecutablePathName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var attempt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxattempt")
	var fitTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxfit")
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxgrow")
	var unavailable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxnone")
	var copyOut = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxcopy")

	// The buffer and its capacity are the loop's carried pair — the phi model is the only way a value
	// crosses a merge at this tier, and `attempt` is the only block with more than one predecessor.
	let buffer = ids.mint()
	let capacity = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(attempt, values: twoArgs(buffer, second: capacity))

	let firstSize = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: MaxPathBytes, ids: ids)
	let firstBuf = emitRawAlloc(module, block: entry, byteCount: firstSize, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(attempt.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(attempt.id, argIds: twoArgs(firstBuf, second: firstSize)))

	// ⚠ `fitTest`, `grow`, `unavailable` and `copyOut` carry NO block args and take no edge arguments:
	// `attempt` dominates all four (it is the only way into any of them), so `buffer`, `capacity` and
	// `written` are simply in scope — the dominance argument `emitCStringLength` states for its own length.
	let written = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(attempt, op: StdOp.osExecutablePath(written, bufLenId: capacity, bufPtrId: buffer))
	// The three-way classification is `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.emitFilledBufferVerdict`'s, not a second copy
	// of it: `GetModuleFileNameA` and `GetCurrentDirectoryA` disagree about what "too small" MEANS (nSize
	// against the size needed) and about what to do next, and agree exactly about how to TELL the three
	// answers apart — including that `written == 0` must be asked before the fit test, because a 0 also
	// satisfies `< capacity`. No edges are pushed here: `attempt` dominates all four exits, so the pair it
	// carries is simply in scope and nothing crosses these merges.
	emitFilledBufferVerdict(module, block: attempt, check: fitTest, written: written, capacity: capacity, whenRefused: unavailable, whenFits: copyOut, whenTooSmall: grow, ids: ids)

	// Truncated: release this attempt's buffer, double the capacity and go round again.
	emitRawFree(module, block: grow, ptr: buffer, ids: ids)
	let shift = emitConst(module, block: grow, value: PathGrowthShift, ids: ids)
	let grownCapacity = emitBin(module, block: grow, a: capacity, b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let grownBuf = emitRawAlloc(module, block: grow, byteCount: grownCapacity, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(grow, op: StdOp.branch(attempt.id))
	grow.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(attempt.id, argIds: twoArgs(grownBuf, second: grownCapacity)))

	// Unavailable: make the buffer the empty string and take the ordinary copy-out.
	let emptyAt = emitConst(module, block: unavailable, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyByte = emitConst(module, block: unavailable, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: unavailable, base: buffer, index: emptyAt, value: emptyByte, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unavailable, op: StdOp.branch(copyOut.id))

	let record = ids.mint()
	var built = emitCStringToManaged(module, func: func, cur: copyOut, cstr: buffer, record: record, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: built, ptr: buffer, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(built, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildProcessExecutablePath'

// ⭐⭐ **`__proc_pid() -> i64`** — the OS-assigned id of the running process, and the whole body is the op.
//
// ⭐ **THE Std OP AND ITS x64 LOWERING ALREADY EXISTED; ONLY THE DOOR WAS MISSING.** `StdOp.osGetPid` and
// `lowerOsGetPid`'s `GetCurrentProcessId` import shipped with the P1.5 IOCP substrate, where the pid is the
// high half of the overlapped pipe name's uniquifier (`GtRuntime.emitGtPipeNameUniquifier`) — so this rung
// adds a name and an entry point, not an instruction. That is why there is no backend work here and no new
// `IatSlot`: a second producer of the same op reaches the same slot.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO RETRY, NO FAILURE ARM AND NO BUFFER**, which is what makes it three lines beside
// `__proc_exe_path`'s loop: `GetCurrentProcessId` reads the PEB, cannot fail, has no out-parameter and
// answers the same DWORD on every call in a process. `isPure` is nevertheless `false` on the op (it is a
// call, and the backends need the call barrier's clobber accounting), so two calls in one function are two
// calls — which `specs-shv2/process-id.md:is-stable-across-calls` asserts agree rather than assuming.
function buildProcessCurrentPid(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ProcessCurrentPidName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	let pid = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osGetPid(pid))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(pid))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildProcessCurrentPid'

// ⭐⭐ **`__proc_bg_priority() -> i64`** — drop this process to background scheduling priority and answer
// what the OS then reports, and, like `__proc_pid`, the whole body is the op.
//
// ⭐ **WHY A PROCESS-WIDE CALL AND NOT A PER-THREAD OR PER-CHILD ONE.** A child inherits the priority class
// on Windows and the nice value under POSIX, so ONE call at a harness's startup covers every worker
// subprocess it spawns AND every test binary those workers launch. A per-child variant would be the same
// fact spelled once per spawn site, and would miss the threads this process runs itself.
//
// ⚠ **THE ANSWER IS A SECOND READING, NOT AN ECHO** — see `StdOp.osEnterBackgroundPriority`, which owns
// that argument and the measurement behind it.
function buildProcessBackgroundPriority(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ProcessBackgroundPriorityName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	let priority = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osEnterBackgroundPriority(priority))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(priority))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildProcessBackgroundPriority'

// ⚠ **ONE GUARD PER ENTRY, NOT ONE FOR THE FILE.** See the header: the three entries cost wildly different
// amounts and a program almost always wants exactly one of them.
export function installProcessRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if usage.usesExecutablePath 'executablePath'
		buildProcessExecutablePath(module)
	end 'executablePath'

	if usage.usesProcessId 'processId'
		buildProcessCurrentPid(module)
	end 'processId'

	if usage.usesBackgroundPriority 'backgroundPriority'
		buildProcessBackgroundPriority(module)
	end 'backgroundPriority'
end 'installProcessRuntime'

// Every callee this file installs — what `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses on a non-Windows
// target. A PREFIX test rather than an `equals` against a roster of names, for `isCommandLineRuntimeCallee`'s
// reason: a second entry point here is then covered by construction instead of having to be remembered at
// two sites.
//
// ✅ **THAT PROMISE WAS TESTED AND HELD.** `__proc_pid` arrived after this predicate was written and needed
// no edit here: `specs-shv2/process-id.md:rejected-on-wasm` pins the E3104 it earns by prefix alone.
//
// ⚠ **`scanRuntimeUsage` DOES NOT READ THIS ONE**, and used to: it now asks for each entry by NAME, because
// the entries are installed on separate bits (see the header). A prefix is the right test for "may this
// target run it at all" — one answer for the family — and the wrong test for "must this program carry it",
// which is per entry point.
export function isProcessRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ProcessRuntimePrefix)
end 'isProcessRuntimeCallee'
